NAME
    chmod - change a file's permission bits

SYNOPSIS
    chmod <octal-mode> <file>

DESCRIPTION
    Sets the permission bits of <file> to <octal-mode>, an octal number such
    as 755, 0644, or 600. Only the low 12 bits are used (rwx for owner/group/
    other, plus setuid/setgid/sticky); the file's type is preserved, so chmod
    can never turn a directory into a file.

    Numeric modes only - there is no symbolic form (u+x) yet.

    ext2 ONLY. Permission bits are an ext2 concept; on FAT32, exFAT, or /proc
    chmod reports "not supported by this filesystem" rather than pretend to
    succeed. This matches ls -l, which shows real modes on ext2 and a
    synthesized placeholder elsewhere.

EXAMPLES
    chmod 755 /bin/prog
    chmod 644 notes.txt
    chmod 700 secret

SEE ALSO
    chown, ls, stat
